    "body": "> [!WARNING]\n> **This is a proof of concept and is not close to done.** It is not intended to\n> merge as it stands. Its purpose is to show that modular implicits fit Hazel's\n> module system and to make one design concrete enough to argue about. The\n> syntax, the resolution rule and the error reporting should all be expected to\n> change. Please read \"Known gaps\" at the bottom before reviewing anything else,\n> and treat everything above it as a sketch rather than a proposal.\n\nEighth of the stack, on top of #2503. A function parameter component can be\ndeclared implicit, and a call that leaves it out has it filled in by the type\nchecker from the instances in scope.\n\n```\ntype SHOW = { type T; let show : T -> String } in\nlet implicit ShowInt = { type T = Int; let show = string_of_int } in\nlet implicit ShowBool = { type T = Bool; let show = fun b -> if b then \"true\" else \"false\" } in\nlet show = fun (implicit S : SHOW, x : S.T) -> S.show(x) in\n   -- show : (implicit S : SHOW, S.T) -> String\nshow(3) ++ show(true)     -- \"3true\": S resolved to ShowInt, then ShowBool\nshow(ShowBool, true)      -- passed by hand: the arity includes S\n```\n\n## What it does\n\n- `implicit S : SIG` is a pattern form (`Pat.Implicit`) and, in an arrow\n  domain, a type form (`Typ.Implicit`). Functions stay uncurried: the binder is\n  a component of the parameter, and it scopes over the later components and the\n  codomain, so `(implicit S : SHOW, S.T) -> String` is an ordinary arrow type.\n- `let implicit M = ...`, and the same as a module item, declare an instance\n  (`Ctx.ImplicitEntry`). Only such bindings take part in resolution. A binder is\n  itself an instance inside the function body, so implicit functions compose.\n- At a call, arity decides: the explicit arity resolves every implicit, the full\n  arity passes them by hand, and a mix is disambiguated by signature. Resolution\n  keeps the instances whose type fits the signature, then narrows by the\n  explicit arguments' types and the expected type. One left is the instance;\n  none or several is a mark on the application, with a hole passed in the\n  instance's place so evaluation proceeds around the error.\n- The instance is spliced into the elaborated argument, so the runtime sees an\n  ordinary application and the stepper shows `show(ShowInt, 3)`.\n- A failed resolution names what the call required, not just the signature:\n  `No implicit instance of SHOW with T = String for S`.\n\nNew module `src/language/statics/Implicits.re` holds the resolution; `docs/modules.md`\nhas a section on it, and the new \"Module Implicits\" doc slide is the worked example.\n\n## Known gaps\n\nDesign questions left open, roughly in the order I would want them settled:\n\n1. **Gradual typing weakens resolution.** An instance member written without an\n   annotation types as `? -> String`, which fits every `{ let show : X -> String }`,\n   so instances become interchangeable and calls turn ambiguous. The workaround\n   in the docs (annotate members, use a manifest `type T = E.T`) is a workaround,\n   not an answer. This is the part I trust least.\n2. **A plain module parameter still loses the caller's type equation**, though\n   it no longer loses it silently. #2498 now closes the parameter as well as\n   the body, so the same program is an error rather than an unchecked call:\n\n   ```\n   let dup = fun (m : SHOW, x : m.T) -> (x, x) in\n   let out : (String, String) = dup(ShowInt, 3) in out\n   -- m.T escaped the scope of its module, so nothing here can have that type;\n      mark the parameter implicit, or annotate it with a manifest signature\n\n   let dup = fun (implicit S : SHOW, x : S.T) -> (x, x) in\n   let out : (String, String) = dup(ShowInt, 3) in out\n   -- error: Expecting type String, String but got inconsistent type\n      ShowInt.T, ShowInt.T\n   ```\n\n   An implicit binder is the only type in the language that can hold what the\n   caller knows, which is why the second version checks and the first only\n   reports its loss. Marking the parameter implicit is a workaround; the fix\n   is a dependent domain for ordinary module parameters, the way Scala's\n   dependent method types work, reusing the scoping and substitution this PR\n   adds, or an ML sharing constraint. Three tests in\n   `Test_Statics_Implicits` pin both halves.\n\n\n3. **Error attribution.** Both resolution marks sit on the application. That was\n   a deliberate choice and it still reads oddly in at least one case on the doc\n   slide. Narrowing also runs only when more than one candidate fits the\n   signature, so a single unfitting instance reports an argument mismatch\n   (`expecting ShowInt.T, got String`) instead of a missing instance. The\n   candidate list in an error is every instance in scope rather than the ones\n   that fit the signature, and the ambiguity message never says that nothing in\n   the call constrained the binder.\n4. **No implicit functors.** Instances cannot themselves take implicit\n   parameters, so there is no `SHOW` for `[T]` derived from `SHOW` for `T` and no\n   recursive search. That is the main thing modular implicits are for, and it is\n   entirely absent.\n5. **Not supported anywhere else.** `DeferredAp` with implicit components;\n   resolving an implicit function that is merely mentioned rather than applied;\n   any spelling of `module implicit M : S = ...`.\n6. **Cost.** The argument is type-checked twice at an implicit call (a probe pass\n   decides the arity), so those applications pay double.\n7. **Syntax warts.** `implicit` is now a keyword. A lone\n   `implicit S : SIG -> \u03c4` needs parentheses in both the editor and the text\n   grammar. The keyword needs two different tile shapes, one per sort, for\n   precedence reasons.\n8. **Type equality is inconsistent about binder names.** `meet`/`ana_meet`\n   rename binders positionally, while `Equality` and `equal_up_to_aliases`\n   compare them literally, so `fast_equal` distinguishes types that are\n   consistent.\n9. **No instance coherence check.** Nothing notices that two instances\n   overlap; you find out at a call site, as an ambiguity. A canonicity or\n   orphan rule would move that error to the declaration, where it belongs.\n\nTwo pre-existing bugs are fixed on the way, both in commit 1: the editor read\n`A -> M.T` as `(A -> M).T` because the type-level dot bound looser than the\narrow, and the pattern-annotation printer was not idempotent (`x:(S.T)`\nreprinted as `x:((S.T))`).\n\n## Verification\n\nEditor behavior was checked by hand; anything that turned up is tracked\noutside this PR.\n\n\n`make dev` clean \u00b7 `make test-quick` 3840 green \u00b7 `Statics.Properties`,\n`MenhirFuzz` (several seeds), `MenhirCorpus` and the reparse suites green \u00b7\n`hazel analyze`/`run` on a 14-function example program and on the new doc slide.\nNew suites: `Statics.Implicits` (39), `Evaluator.Implicits` (15),\n`Typ.Implicit` (9), `Editing.Implicit` (4), plus Menhir, MakeTerm, ExpToSegment,\nElaboration, Equality, Abbreviate and ExplainThis cases.\n\n## Stack note\n\nParts 3 to 7 were force-pushed with this: a Coverage crash on `case m.x` with an\nabstract `m.T` is fixed where it was introduced (#2495, \"a stuck path type is\nopaque, not non-normalized\") and cascaded up the stack. No other change to those\nbranches.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n",
